            <ArticleTitle>Dengue Fever Triggering Kawasaki Disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>51-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Several bacterial and viral infections are listed as triggering factors for Kawasaki disease; association with dengue fever is rare.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE CHARACTERISTICS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">A 5-year-old girl who presented with fever that was confirmed to be dengue fever, and subsequently improved, except that the fever persisted. She fulfilled diagnostic criteria for Kawasaki disease on day 7 of fever.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Child responded satisfactorily to intravenous immunoglobulin administration.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MESSAGE" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Kawasaki disease should be kept as one of the probabilities in a case of dengue if fever persists beyond the expected duration.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
